---
intent: >
  Deliver a re-runnable TypeScript script that consolidates every AIDE methodology
  artifact into a single copyright-registrable PDF with cover page, TOC, rendered
  markdown body, running footers, and correct page numbering.
---

## Plan

### 1. Install dependencies and add the npm script

- [x] Install three runtime dependencies as **devDependencies** (this is a build-time script, not shipped code): `puppeteer`, `marked`, `pdf-lib`. Command: `npm install --save-dev puppeteer marked pdf-lib @types/marked`.
- [x] Add an npm script to `package.json` under `"scripts"`: `"export-pdf": "tsx scripts/export-pdf.ts"`. This uses `tsx` (already available via vitest's transitive dep) to run TypeScript directly without a separate compile step.
- [x] Verify the script entry runs without error (it will fail until step 2 creates the file — this step only wires the plumbing).

**Convention notes for implementor:**
- `devDependencies`, not `dependencies` — this script is not part of the published MCP server.
- The script lives at `scripts/export-pdf.ts` — a standalone file, NOT inside `src/`. It is a utility script, not a module in the server's domain. It does not follow the orchestrator/helper pattern because it is not a service module — it is a single-purpose build script with no consumers other than the CLI.

---

### 2. Create the export-pdf script — file discovery and markdown collection

- [x] Create `scripts/export-pdf.ts`.
- [x] Define the four source directory constants relative to project root. Use `import.meta.dirname` (Node 22+) or `path.resolve` from `process.cwd()` to get the project root. The four directories are:
  - `.aide/docs/` — methodology docs
  - `.claude/agents/aide/` — agent definitions
  - `.claude/commands/aide/` — command definitions
  - `.claude/skills/` — skill definitions (contains subdirectories `brain/` and `study-playbook/`)
- [x] Define the four section names as display labels: `"Methodology Docs"`, `"Agent Definitions"`, `"Command Definitions"`, `"Skill Definitions"`.
- [x] For each directory, glob for `*.md` files. For the skills directory, also recurse into subdirectories (`**/*.md`). Use `node:fs` `readdirSync` + `node:path` — no external glob library needed since the directory structure is shallow and known.
- [x] Sort discovered files alphabetically by filename within each section. For skills, group subdirectory files after top-level files, sorted by subdirectory name then filename.
- [x] For each file, read the markdown content. Extract the first `# heading` line as the display name for the TOC. If no H1 exists, derive the display name from the filename (strip `.md`, convert kebab-case to Title Case).
- [x] Build an in-memory data structure: an array of sections, each with a `label` (section name), and an array of `documents`, each with `displayName` and `markdownContent`.

**Convention notes for implementor:**
- Use `node:fs` and `node:path` imports (Node protocol prefix).
- Use `const` with immediate assignment, not `let` with later mutation.
- Name functions by what they DO: `discoverFiles`, `readSection`, `extractDisplayName` — verb-first.
- JSDoc on every function, even in a script — explain *why* it exists, not what the signature already says.
- Early returns over nesting for guard clauses.

---

### 3. Build the HTML document — cover page, TOC, and rendered body

- [x] Define the five cover page constants as string literals (these are legal metadata and must not be derived):
  - Title: `"The AIDEMD Methodology Specification v1.0"`
  - Author: `"Jacob Carpenter"`
  - Entity: `"TetsuKodai Group LLC"`
  - Copyright: `"\u00a9 2026 TetsuKodai Group LLC. All rights reserved."`
  - Publication date: `"April 12, 2026"`
- [x] Build the cover page HTML as a `<section>` with `page-break-after: always`. Center content vertically using flexbox. The cover has no footer — it will be rendered as a separate PDF in step 4.
- [x] Build the table of contents HTML. For each section, render the section label as an `<h2>`, then each document's `displayName` as an indented list item. The TOC is generated from the same data structure built in step 2 — this guarantees TOC/body synchronization (spec desired outcome #2 and undesired outcome #2).
- [x] Add a `page-break-after: always` after the TOC section.
- [x] For each section, render:
  - A section heading (`<h1>` with the section label)
  - For each document: an `<h2>` with the display name, then the markdown rendered to HTML via `marked`. Add `page-break-before: always` on each document's `<h2>` so each document starts on a new page.
- [x] Configure `marked` with default options. It handles code fences, tables, and nested lists out of the box.
- [x] Wrap everything in a full HTML document (`<!DOCTYPE html><html>...`) with an embedded `<style>` block for typography:
  - Body: `font-family: Georgia, serif; line-height: 1.6; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px;`
  - Code blocks: `font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; background: #f5f5f5; padding: 12px; border-radius: 4px; overflow-x: auto; page-break-inside: avoid;`
  - Inline code: `background: #f0f0f0; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 0.9em;`
  - Tables: `border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;` with `border: 1px solid #ddd; padding: 8px;` on `th`/`td`. `page-break-inside: avoid` on `<table>`.
  - Headings: reasonable sizes, `page-break-after: avoid` on `h1`/`h2` to keep them with their content.
  - The cover section: `display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center; height: 100vh; text-align: center;`

**Convention notes for implementor:**
- Name the function that builds the full HTML `buildHtml` (verb-first).
- Name sub-functions: `buildCoverHtml`, `buildTocHtml`, `buildBodyHtml`, `renderMarkdown`.
- All helper functions defined ABOVE the main orchestrating function in the file.
- Use template literals for HTML construction — no template engine dependency.

---

### 4. Two-pass PDF generation and merge

- [x] Import `puppeteer` and `pdf-lib` (`PDFDocument` from `pdf-lib`).
- [x] Create the output directory `dist/` if it does not exist (`node:fs` `mkdirSync` with `recursive: true`).
- [x] **Pass 1 — Cover PDF:** Launch Puppeteer, load the full HTML, but print only page 1 (the cover) with `page.pdf({ pageRanges: '1' })`. No `displayHeaderFooter`. This produces a cover PDF buffer with no footer or page number.
- [x] **Pass 2 — Body PDF:** Load the HTML again but this time set `displayHeaderFooter: true` with:
  - `headerTemplate`: empty `<span></span>` (required by Puppeteer even if unused)
  - `footerTemplate`: `<div style="font-size: 9px; width: 100%; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 0 40px;"><span>\u00a9 2026 TetsuKodai Group LLC. All rights reserved.</span><span>Page <span class="pageNumber"></span></span></div>`
  - `margin`: `{ top: '40px', bottom: '60px', left: '40px', right: '40px' }` (bottom margin accommodates footer)
  - `pageRanges`: `'2-'` (skip the cover page, print from page 2 onward — this is the body with TOC + content)
  - `format`: `'Letter'`
- [x] **Merge:** Use `pdf-lib` to merge the two PDF buffers:
  - Load both buffers as `PDFDocument` instances via `PDFDocument.load()`.
  - Create a new `PDFDocument`, copy all pages from the cover PDF, then copy all pages from the body PDF using `copyPages` + `addPage`.
  - Save the merged document to `dist/aide-methodology.pdf`.
- [x] Close the Puppeteer browser instance in a `finally` block to avoid zombie Chrome processes.
- [x] Print the absolute output path to stdout: `console.log('PDF generated: ' + outputPath)`.

**Convention notes for implementor:**
- Name functions: `generateCoverPdf`, `generateBodyPdf`, `mergePdfs`, and the top-level orchestrator `exportPdf`.
- The orchestrator `exportPdf` is the default-export-equivalent (since this is a script, it is the `main()` at the bottom of the file that calls `exportPdf()`).
- Use `await` with `try/finally` for browser cleanup — not `.then()` chains.
- Use `Uint8Array` for PDF buffers (pdf-lib's native type).

---

### 5. Wire the main entry point and verify end-to-end

- [x] At the bottom of `scripts/export-pdf.ts`, add the top-level invocation: call `exportPdf()` and handle rejection with `console.error` + `process.exit(1)`.
- [x] Run `npm run export-pdf` and verify:
  - The script completes without error.
  - `dist/aide-methodology.pdf` exists.
  - The PDF opens with a clean cover page (no footer, no page number).
  - Page 2+ has the TOC followed by body content.
  - Every page after the cover has the copyright footer and page numbers.
  - Code fences render as styled blocks, not raw markdown.
  - Tables have borders.
  - All four sections are present with all files from each source directory.
- [x] If any source file fails to parse, the script must throw (not silently skip). This prevents the TOC/body desync described in the spec's bad examples.

**Convention notes for implementor:**
- No `console.log` in the body of the script except the final success message with the output path. Use `console.error` for errors only.
- The script is synchronous top-to-bottom: discover files, build HTML, generate PDFs, merge, write, print path, exit.

---

## Decisions

**Single file, not orchestrator/helper pattern.** The spec explicitly calls this "a utility, not a product feature." It has no consumers, no runtime environment, and no reason to optimize for modularity. A single `scripts/export-pdf.ts` file with well-named internal functions is the right granularity. Splitting into subfolders would be over-engineering for a script that runs once and exits. The progressive disclosure orchestrator/helper pattern is for service modules with reusable parts — this is neither.

**`tsx` over `ts-node`.** The project already has `tsx` available transitively through vitest. `tsx` is faster (esbuild-based) and handles ESM + TypeScript without tsconfig gymnastics. No additional install needed.

**`devDependencies` for puppeteer, marked, pdf-lib.** These are build-time tools for generating the PDF. They must not ship with the published npm package. The `"files"` field in `package.json` already excludes `scripts/` from the published bundle.

**`marked` over `markdown-it`.** Both are capable. `marked` is lighter, has zero dependencies, and its defaults handle code fences, tables, and nested lists — exactly what the spec requires. No plugins needed. `markdown-it` is more extensible but that extensibility is wasted here.

**Two-pass PDF with pdf-lib merge over single-pass tricks.** Puppeteer cannot conditionally suppress the footer on page 1. The spec requires the cover page to have no footer while all subsequent pages carry the copyright footer and page number. Generating two PDFs (cover without footer, body with footer) and merging them with pdf-lib is the cleanest solution — exactly as the spec's strategy section prescribes.

**`dist/aide-methodology.pdf` output path.** `dist/` is already the project's build output directory (tsconfig `outDir`). Placing the PDF here keeps generated artifacts in one place and `dist/` is already gitignored.

**Dynamic file discovery via `readdirSync`, not hardcoded lists.** The spec explicitly calls out hardcoded file lists as an undesired outcome. Globbing each directory at runtime ensures new files are automatically included.

**No tests for the script.** This is a single-purpose build utility with no consumers, no API surface, and no business logic. Its correctness is verified by opening the PDF. Writing Vitest tests for Puppeteer PDF generation would require mocking the entire browser and PDF library, producing tests that verify mock behavior rather than actual output. The manual verification in step 5 is the appropriate quality gate.

**`node:fs` and `node:path` over glob libraries.** The directory structure is shallow and well-known. Four directories, one with two subdirectories. Native `readdirSync` with a simple filter is clearer and avoids an additional dependency.
